{
  "id": "separable-variables-circles",
  "title": "Separable Variables: The Circle Family",
  "eyebrow": "first-order ODEs",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 71,
  "topic": "Separable first-order ODE technique — for dy/dx = -x/y, move all y's to one side and all x's to the other, integrate each side, and the family of solutions is a one-parameter family of circles x² + y² = C centred at the origin.",
  "subject_id": "mat2b",
  "chapter_number": 4,
  "prerequisites": [],
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "manimoIntro",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 7,
      "narration": "Sometimes the x's and the y's want to live on different sides of an equation. When they do, integration is easy.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "manimo-enter",
            "content": "Manimo bobs in centre-screen",
            "delay": 0
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Separate, then integrate.",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "theOde",
      "start": 7,
      "end": 21.36,
      "narration": "Take the equation d y by d x equals minus x over y. At every point, the slope is minus x over y — a tangent that turns with the position. What family of curves matches that slope everywhere?",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "left-right",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "graph-axes",
            "content": "x-y plane",
            "delay": 0.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Slope-field strokes at a grid of points, slope = -x/y at each",
            "delay": 0.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "dy/dx = -x/y",
            "delay": 1.5,
            "color": "var(--violet-400)",
            "position": "right panel"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "what curves have this slope everywhere?",
            "delay": 2.5,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "below formula"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "separate",
      "start": 21.36,
      "end": 31.89,
      "narration": "Cross-multiply: y d y equals minus x d x. Every y has crossed to the left side, every x to the right. The variables are separated.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "dy/dx = -x/y",
            "delay": 0,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "y dy = -x dx",
            "delay": 1.5,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "note": "animate y sliding to the left, x to the right"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "variables separated — each side depends on only one letter",
            "delay": 3,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "integrate",
      "start": 31.89,
      "end": 46.61,
      "narration": "Now integrate both sides. The integral of y d y is y squared over two; the integral of minus x d x is minus x squared over two. Bundle the constants: x squared plus y squared equals C.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "∫ y dy = ∫ -x dx",
            "delay": 0,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "y²/2 = -x²/2 + C₁",
            "delay": 1.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "x² + y² = C",
            "delay": 3,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "centre payoff"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "one constant — one parameter family",
            "delay": 4.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "circleFamily",
      "start": 46.61,
      "end": 60.27,
      "narration": "And the family is circles, centred at the origin. Pick a value for C; that picks one circle. Slide C, and the whole family materialises — each curve riding its own slope field tangent at every point.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "left-right",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "svg-path",
            "content": "Slope-field strokes for dy/dx = -x/y",
            "delay": 0,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-circle",
            "content": "Active circle of radius sqrt(C), C swept from 0.5 to 4",
            "delay": 0.5,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)",
            "note": "GENUINE MOTION: scrub C, circle grows; tangent ticks always agree with circle slope"
          },
          {
            "type": "svg-circle",
            "content": "Trail of all visited C values, drawn as faint amber arcs",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "x² + y² = C",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "right panel"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "C grows → circle grows",
            "delay": 1,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "takeaway",
      "start": 60.27,
      "end": 71,
      "narration": "If you can factor the equation so x's live with d x and y's live with d y, you can integrate side by side. That is the separable trick.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "f(y) dy = g(x) dx  →  integrate both sides",
            "delay": 0.3,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "(works when the ODE factors into a y-piece and an x-piece)",
            "delay": 1.6,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
